  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "71528c49",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "- We have a total of 8 428 152 biketrips in Oslo, Trondheim and Bergen.\n",
    "- The columns in the Bike-sharing dataset include: `started_at`, `endend_at`, `duration`, `start_station_id`, `start_station_name`, `start_station_description`, `start_station_latitude`, `start_station_longitude`, `end_station_id`, `end_station_name`, `end_station_description`, `end_station_latitude`, `end_station_longitude`. \n",
    "\n",
    "#### Data Types\n",
    "The 13 columns are stored as string data types. This is because PySpark reads CSV columns as strings by default. However, Several columns represent numeric values or timestamps. We will therefore convert these columns to appropriate data types. \n",
    "\n",
    "- Timestamps: `started_at`and `ended_at` -> timestamp\n",
    "- Trip duration: `duration` -> integer(maybe double?)\n",
    "- Coorinates: `latitude`and `longitude`-> double\n",
    "\n",
    "Maybe:\n",
    "- StationIDs: `start_station_id` and `stop_station_id` -> keep as string og convert to integer\n",
    "\n",
    "From the table above we can see the ID is stored as a numeric value, but we would argue to keep them as a string as they are identifiers rather than numeric measurements. This also provides a greater flexibility if future datasets contains letters(e.g. ST123), without requiring changes to the data type. \n"
   ]
  },
  {
